{"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/SwiftTimecodeAV\/documentation\/SwiftTimecodeAV\/AVAsset-Timecode-Track"},"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"level":2,"anchor":"overview","type":"heading","text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"SwiftTimecode provides a full suite of methods to read and write timecode track(s) in a QuickTime movie file when working with AVFoundation."}]},{"level":2,"anchor":"Read-Timecode-from-QuickTime-Movie","type":"heading","text":"Read Timecode from QuickTime Movie"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Simple methods to read start timecode and duration from ","type":"text"},{"code":"AVAsset","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and its subclasses (","type":"text"},{"code":"AVMovie","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") as well as ","type":"text"},{"code":"AVAssetTrack","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" are provided by SwiftTimecode.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"The methods are throwing since timecode information is not guaranteed to be present inside movie files.","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let asset = AVAsset( ... )"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Auto-detect the movie’s frame rate (if it’s embedded in the file) and return it if desired."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"If frame rate information is not available in the video file, this method will throw an error."}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let frameRate = try await asset.timecodeFrameRate() \/\/ ie: .fps29_97"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Read the start timecode, duration expressed as elapsed timecode, and end timecode."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"If a known frame rate is not passed to the methods, the frame rate will be auto-detected.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"If frame rate information is not available in the video file, 